WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:02.134
London Burning A simple mistake ignited one of the worst fires in world history. Sometime in the

2
00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:07.033
early hours of Sunday, September 2, 1666, a baker named Thomas Farriner went to sleep in London,

3
00:00:07.034 --> 00:00:18.854
England, without extinguishing the fires in his oven. In the middle of the night, the oven sparked

4
00:00:18.855 --> 00:00:23.026
a flame in the kitchen. Moments later the fire was spreading quickly through his house on Pudding

5
00:00:23.027 --> 00:00:27.944
Lane. All the conditions were right that day for a terrible conflagration. Within this old city, wooden

6
00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:32.020
buildings stood close together along narrow, winding, and crowded streets. The summer had been hot and dry,

7
00:00:32.021 --> 00:00:38.686
and on September 2, a strong wind was blowing. Fanned by these gusty winds, the fire rapidly

8
00:00:38.687 --> 00:00:44.763
consumed one building after another. By midnight it had reached warehouses along the riverfront where highly flammable

9
00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:50.834
substances, such as wine, oil, tar, and coal, further fueled its flames. In the hours and

10
00:00:50.835 --> 00:01:02.489
days to follow, the fire engulfed homes, shops, schools, prisons, and entire neighborhoods. It destroyed nearly ninety

11
00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:10.171
churches, including St. Paul’s Cathedral, one of the oldest and grandest buildings in the city. The heat

12
00:01:10.172 --> 00:01:18.140
from the fire was so intense that it melted glass, coins, lead—even the thick iron bars of

13
00:01:18.141 --> 00:01:27.309
a city prison. By seven o'clock that first morning, three hundred houses had already burned to the

14
00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:33.395
ground. By the time the fire was completely extinguished—4 days later—nearly 80 percent of the city was

15
00:01:33.396 --> 00:01:41.116
in ashes. The Human Toll As the Great Fire continued to burn, many Londoners, including the king's

16
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:42.941
mother, Henrietta Maria, fled to places outside the city. Those who could afford to move had their

17
00:01:42.942 --> 00:01:52.284
possessions taken from their homes and put on barges that floated to safer areas. Some people left

18
00:01:52.285 --> 00:01:59.450
London and never returned. Thousands of people camped around the city's outskirts in tents and flimsy shacks

19
00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:02.937
with family members and whatever possessions they had left. A well-known writer named John Evelyn wrote that,

20
00:02:02.938 --> 00:02:11.525
in these refugee camps, he saw "poor inhabitants . . . many without a rag or any

21
00:02:11.526 --> 00:02:26.515
necessary utensils, bed, or board; who . . . were now reduced to . . . misery

22
00:02:26.516 --> 00:02:29.936
and poverty." Most people did not know what had caused the fire. Citizens roamed the streets looking

23
00:02:29.937 --> 00:02:35.579
for arsonists who might have deliberately started the blaze. Foreigners were blamed too. Those from Holland or

24
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:44.336
France found themselves in particular danger because England was at war with those countries. One day, as

25
00:02:44.337 --> 00:02:53.400
the Great Fire burned, a mob of angry Londoners prowled the streets, looking for foreigners—and then killing

26
00:02:53.401 --> 00:02:57.476
any they found. It is not known precisely how many people perished in the fire. Information,

27
00:02:57.477 --> 00:03:01.981
especially about poor people, was not carefully recorded. But experts agree that thousands lost their homes and

28
00:03:01.982 --> 00:03:06.307
belongings. For many of those left homeless, exposure to cold weather soon led to illness and death.

29
00:03:06.308 --> 00:03:11.985
Fighting the Fire Fire prevention and fire-fighting techniques in the 1600s were not very sophisticated. The city

30
00:03:11.986 --> 00:03:13.969
didn't have a fire department so people worked together to put out any fires that sprung up,

31
00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:19.874
mostly using buckets of water to douse the flames. The law required the creation of firebreaks, or

32
00:03:19.875 --> 00:03:29.215
open spaces between buildings, to prevent fires from spreading easily. But in London, the Lord Mayor, Sir

33
00:03:29.216 --> 00:03:37.296
Thomas Bloodworth, had hesitated to spend the money necessary to enforce this law. And by the time

34
00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:45.368
Bloodworth finally ordered that buildings be demolished to make firebreaks—on Sunday, September 2—it was far too late.

35
00:03:45.369 --> 00:03:54.363
King Charles put his brother, James, in charge of fighting the blaze. James organized teams of men

36
00:03:54.364 --> 00:03:59.022
who knocked down buildings to try to starve the fire of fuel. The king and his brother

37
00:03:59.023 --> 00:04:05.269
also rode around the city on horseback, urging people to pitch in and offering rewards to those

38
00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:13.183
who joined in the fight. Later both men were praised for their efforts. By late Wednesday, September

39
00:04:13.184 --> 00:04:16.183
5, the wind had died down, and many of the firebreaks had stopped the fire from spreading

40
00:04:16.184 --> 00:04:24.252
further. The next morning only small fires burned here and there. The Great Fire of London was

41
00:04:24.253 --> 00:04:31.155
finally over. Recovering and Rebuilding For Londoners, the recovery process was long and difficult. After the fire

42
00:04:31.156 --> 00:04:38.154
many people felt traumatized by its devastating effects. A famous writer named Samuel Pepys, who kept a

43
00:04:38.155 --> 00:04:43.169
diary during the fire, described his recurring nightmares. Like many others, he was "much terrified in the

44
00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:50.564
nights . . . with dreams of fire and falling down of houses." King Charles continued

45
00:04:50.565 --> 00:04:57.811
trying to help the citizens of London after the fire was put out. He ordered that bread

46
00:04:57.812 --> 00:05:03.142
be distributed for free. He set up courts to resolve housing disputes that broke out after the

47
00:05:03.143 --> 00:05:11.635
fire between tenants and landlords. He also asked for the public's ideas on the best way to

48
00:05:11.636 --> 00:05:17.371
rebuild the ruined city. People had many different ideas about what to do. Finally, after great debate,

49
00:05:17.372 --> 00:05:20.617
it was decided that London would be rebuilt according to its former street plan. But unlike the

50
00:05:20.618 --> 00:05:27.446
former plan, the new plan was created with fire safety in mind. Many streets were widened and

51
00:05:27.447 --> 00:05:33.602
straightened, and new houses were constructed not with wood, but with brick and stone. Planners also made

52
00:05:33.603 --> 00:05:41.268
sure that the Thames River—an important water source for fighting fires—remained accessible. After the Fire The Great

53
00:05:41.269 --> 00:05:47.934
Fire of 1666 was one of the city's worst disasters. The fire burned for four days and

54
00:05:47.935 --> 00:05:55.012
four nights, and over thirteen thousand houses and four hundred acres of land were destroyed. Many people

55
00:05:55.013 --> 00:06:03.505
were left homeless and penniless, having lost everything in the fire. But the tragedy also brought some

56
00:06:03.506 --> 00:06:08.988
benefits for London, as well as for the rest of the world. London was still recovering from

57
00:06:08.989 --> 00:06:14.421
the Great Plague, which had ravaged the city and taken thousands of lives. The fire killed rats

58
00:06:14.422 --> 00:06:21.318
that carried the plague, perhaps helping to finally eradicate the disease. In addition, the Great Fire led

59
00:06:21.319 --> 00:06:27.310
to the development of more sophisticated fire-fighting techniques. Newer cities were also planned with fire safety in

60
00:06:27.311 --> 00:06:33.382
mind. When Englishman William Penn founded the American city of Philadelphia in 1682, he remembered the fire

61
00:06:33.383 --> 00:06:37.644
in London. He made sure the streets in his city plan were more open and the

62
00:06:37.645 --> 00:06:46.047
houses were not packed together too closely. In 1668 Christopher Wren, a famous architect, began making plans

63
00:06:46.048 --> 00:06:52.959
to rebuild St. Paul’s Cathedral on its original site. By 1677 Wren had also erected a monument

64
00:06:52.960 --> 00:07:02.279
to the Great Fire, often just called the Monument. Shaped like a candle with flames at the

65
00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:10.033
top, this tall stone column marks the spot where the fire started. Its north side is etched

66
00:07:10.034 --> 00:07:17.533
with words that recall the terrible blaze that "devoured even distant buildings and rushed . . .

67
00:07:17.534 --> 00:07:29.272
through every quarter with astonishing swiftness and noise." But the Monument is not just a memorial to

68
00:07:29.273 --> 00:07:36.003
history; it has also become a symbol of London's rebirth. Thousands of visitors climb its stairs each

69
00:07:36.004 --> 00:07:44.992
year to look out on the city that, once rebuilt, became the center of a powerful empire.

